ow-it-all, has a hot temper.\n\nWe need to evaluate criteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention, crew cooperation, professional communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nLet's weigh.\n\nCandidate B: strong reliability (conscientious), stress resilience (handles stress well), technical competence (weather assessment, computer skills), but concerns: weak memory for numbers (attention), makes nasty remarks (crew cooperation, professional communication). So mixed.\n\nCandidate C: good decision speed, calm in crisis, technical understanding, but communication difficulty, egocentric, not willing to further education (adaptability). So concerns.\n\nCandidate D: good problem solving, concentration, responsibility, responds to unexpected events, but hot temper, know-it-all (cooperation, communication). So concerns.\n\nCandidate A: anticipates danger, sees complex connections, spatial vision, leadership, but show-off, not open to new ideas (cooperation, adaptability). No explicit stress resilience or reliability noted. Might be strong technical but lacking on cooperation.\n\nWhich candidate overall strongest? B seems balanced but communication issue is serious. C has calmness and decision quality but communication and adaptability issues. D has responsibility and problem solving but hot temper. A has leadership but show-off.\n\nProbably B is best overall despite communication issue; but we need to discuss all candidates. As first contribution, we can propose a provisional recommendation for Candidate B, citing strengths and acknowledging concerns, and suggest we need more info on communication behavior.\n\nWe must also reference that no prior discussion, so we are starting.\n\nThus public message: I propose Candidate B as leading, based on conscientiousness, stress handling, weather assessment, but note concerns about memory and remarks. Suggest we explore further.\n\nMetadata vote: Candidate B.\n\nProceed."}
